---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const matSelCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Translate: design → material requirements', note: '"Light, stiff, cheap, survives 80 °C" becomes property targets: density, modulus, cost per volume, service temperature. Translation is where design engineering and materials engineering shake hands.' },
    { math: '2 · Screening and ranking', note: 'Screen the universe of materials (~100,000 in databases) against hard limits; rank survivors by a performance index — e.g. E^½/ρ for a light stiff beam: the maths of which property combination wins for which job.' },
    { math: '3 · Ashby charts make the trade visible', note: 'Plot property against property (strength vs density, modulus vs cost) on log axes — each material class occupies a bubble. The best material for "light and strong" is the one the 45° line touches first. The whole discipline on one chart.' },
    { math: '4 · Shape, processing and documentation', note: 'Section shape multiplies performance (an I-beam beats a rod of the same mass); processing constrains choice (castable? weldable?); documentation seals the audit trail. The CES selector software industrialised Ashby\'s method.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/advanced/materials-selection">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Materials selection is <strong>the systematic matching of stuff to requirements</strong> —
    Ashby\'s method: translate the design into properties, screen, rank by performance indices,
    and read the trade-offs off his famous bubble charts. It is the engineering lesson the
    materials ladder feeds.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="translate, screen, chart, document" widget="equationStep" config={matSelCfg} />

  <h2>Performance indices in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Light and stiff (aircraft spars):</strong> E/ρ — carbon fibre and aluminium beat steel. Light and stiff in bending: E^½/ρ — wood competes with the metals (the plant-biology lesson\'s evolved engineering).</li>
    <li><strong>Thermal shock resistance (cookware, re-entry):</strong> σ/k·E — why borosilicate glass survives stovetops and soda-lime does not (the chemistry lesson\'s expansion coefficients, ranked).</li>
    <li><strong>Springs store energy per volume:</strong> σ²/E — why spring steel and titanium compete and rubber (huge elastic strain) is a spring by another mechanism (the elasticity of the materials lesson, optimised).</li>
    <li><strong>Sustainability indices exist now:</strong> embodied carbon per unit stiffness — timber rising up the rankings as the index changes, which is the whole method\'s point: change the objective, watch the winner change (the architecture lesson\'s mass-timber wave).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ashby, Materials Selection in Mechanical Design; Granta/CES Edupack; the materials ladder\'s why-materials-behave lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
